{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Special Period''' ({{lang-bl|Período especial}}), also known as the '''Adjustment Period''' ({{lang-bl|Período adequación|link=no}}), was an extended period of [[October 76|political]], {{W|food crisis|food}}, {{W|energy crisis|energy}} and {{W|economic crisis}} in [[Balisca]] that began in late 1976, primarily due the [[Camboriú War]] with [[Potocsí]], the [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis]], and the subsequent {{W|1979 oil crisis}}. The {{W|economic depression}} of the Special Period was at its most severe between 1977 and 1980, but improved thereafter as a result of societal changes and assistance from [[Burawa]], [[Daras]] and members of the [[XX]], whom together transported over ? {{W|tonnes}} of food and other materials to Balisca.

The worst of the crisis coincided with the election of [[Ecological Action|ecologist]] [[Otávia Brito]] to the position of [[Secretary General of Balisca|Secretary General]]. Under her leadership, the government encouraged and later assisted citizens in growing their own food on any available pieces of land. Urban gardens sprang up throughout the country on roof-tops, patios, and unused parking lots in raised beds as well as "squatting" on empty lots. These efforts were furthered by the [[Secretariat of Local Development and Modernization]]'s efforts to teach {{W|permaculture}}, a sustainable agricultural system, and supporting {{W|communal farming}} through {{W|agricultural cooperative}}s, fostering a sense of {{W|communalism|communalism}} of community engagement and {{W|capacity building}}.

Changes during the Special Period included an unprecedented level of responsibility and cooperation at the [[Politics of Balisca|confederal level]] by the [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] and [[Regions of Balisca|regions]], and internationally, the diplomatic realignment of the country towards [[Burawa]] with the [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement]]. This period radically transformed Baliscan society and the economy, as it necessitated the revival of [[Rural society in Balisca|rural Balisca]], a return to {{W|organic agriculture}}, curtailed {{W|meat consumption}}, stunted the growth of [[W:Car ownership|car ownership]] in the country (fuel was prioritized for {{W|diesel locomotive|diesel}} cargo trains), and overhauled domestic industry, health and diets.

Despite the struggles, Balisca made significant strides in reducing dependency on foreign sources for fuel through {{W|ethanol production}}, increased {{W|energy efficiency}}, {{W|renewable energy}} proliferation, and was able to secure new food imports long enough to implemented measures to increase domestic food production. 

